      "program_name": "County-owned land for affordable housing: Dollar Creek Crossing (3205 North Lake Boulevard, Tahoe City)",
      "administering_agency": "Placer County Executive Office, Economic Development and Housing, with the Community Development Resource Agency; developer Related California (Related Norcal Development, LLC) under a preliminary agreement",
      "funding_type": "land",
      "typical_award_size": "County-owned parcels bought in 2019 across from the 7-Eleven on North Lake Boulevard. After seven years of mixed-use and mixed-income concepts, the Board directed an affordable-only project of at least 80 lower income units in April 2025; the developer's June 2026 financial report says the County would need to contribute about $18,500,000 (land plus gap) for feasibility, and the County has not identified a funding source. The preliminary agreement was set to expire at the end of June 2026; if it lapses the County must issue a new RFP no later than July 2027.",
      "per_unit_cap": null,
      "affordability_requirement": "Low income (at or below 80% AMI) rental housing, 80 units, with the County's Affordable Housing Priority Applicant Policy for local workers and residents.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "new construction",
        "supportive housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Watch for a new RFP by July 2027 if the Related agreement expired; the County's Ad Hoc Committee for Housing Funding and Fee Implementation is tasked with finding the $18.5 million (supervisors have floated a bond or other borrowing). A community open house was held April 2, 2026.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "placer.ca.gov (including DocumentCenter PDFs) sits behind Cloudflare and refused automated access on 2026-11-06; Board items were read from the County's GovDelivery agenda attachments (content.govdelivery.com/attachments/CAPLACER), the Legistar client 'placerca', state records and news coverage. Dollar Creek is the County's only land contribution in the Tahoe Basin and shows the limit of its resources: the land is in hand but the County has no fund large enough for an $18.5 million gap, so the deal will depend on state money (MHP, IIG, AHSC, TRPA bonus units) plus TOT programs. A developer answering a future RFP should price the TRPA coverage and allocation process and the County's requirement for workforce preference. County land plus County funds will trigger prevailing wage.",
      "source_url": "https://sierrasun.com/news/tahoe-city-affordable-housing-development-meets-funding-challenges",
